# str() summarised a set as set(iCount + count) -- the LITERAL part only:
#
#     else if (s->kind == 0) snprintf(buf, bufsz, "set(%d)", s->iCount + s->count);
#
# A set literal is a UNION of three parts (the literal integers in i64, the
# literal non-integers in items, and the interval COMPONENTS in comps), so that
# sum is not the cardinality of anything that has a component.  Measured before
# the fix, beside the len()/count() that were already correct (size() is the
# deprecated set-context spelling of count(); see docs/TYPESET_V06.md 7.8):
#
#     str(1, 2, Z[7~9])   -> set(2)   len 5
#     str(Z[1~4])         -> set(0)   len 4      (`z = Z[1~4]` is a NEW_SET
#                                                 over one component)
#     str(Z[1~3], Z[5~7]) -> set(0)   len 6
#     str(1, 2, 3)        -> set(3)   len 3      (no component: already right)
#
# `set(0)` for a four-element set is not a summary, it is a wrong answer: it
# reads as "empty".  str() now enumerates through vm_set_to_array whenever the
# set has a component, exactly as len()/count()/list()/sum() do, so the two agree
# by construction.  Enumeration de-duplicates, because a literal can sit inside
# a component (`1, Z[1~3]` is {1,2,3}, not four) and two components can overlap.
#
# A component that cannot be walked (an endpoint into infinity, a whole named
# set, or more than ten million members) is still refused by the enumerator, and
# then str() and len() both fall back to the literal part -- they agree there
# too.  `Z` on its own is kind 1 and `float1[0~0.3]` is kind 2, neither of which
# ever claimed a count.
#
# See docs/AUDIT.md §1.17.
a = 1, 2, Z[7~9]
b = Z[1~4]
c = 1, Z[1~3]
d = Z[1~3], Z[5~7]
e = 1, 2, 3
f = Z[1~10000001]
say "setstr-ok union=" + str(a) + "/" + str(len(a)) + " single=" + str(b) + "/" + str(len(b)) + " overlap=" + str(c) + "/" + str(len(c)) + " two=" + str(d) + "/" + str(len(d)) + " plain=" + str(e) + "/" + str(len(e)) + " refused=" + str(f) + "/" + str(len(f)) + " named=" + str(Z) + " interval=" + str(float1[0~0.3])
